          "lineContent": "mcpServers[mcp.Name] = serverEntry",
          "title": "MCP entry insertion",
          "description": "Adds the MCP server configuration to the provider's config file"
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "path": "skills/providers/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 359,
          "lineContent": "if err := writeConfig(path, cfg, p.ConfigType); err != nil {",
          "title": "Configuration persistence",
          "description": "Writes the updated configuration back to the provider's config file"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Provider Config Management System\n├── run() entry point \u003c-- 2a\n│   ├── Operation switch statement \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:172\n│   │   ├── add-mcp operation \u003c-- 2c\n│   │   │   ├── getTargetProviders() \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:809\n│   │   │   ├── buildMCPEntry() \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:820\n│   │   │   └── Config manipulation \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:322\n│   │   │       ├── mcpServers[mcp.Name] = entry \u003c-- 2d\n│   │   │       └── writeConfig() persistence \u003c-- 2e\n│   │   └── Other operations (get, set, sync...) \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:173\n│   └── Provider registry access \u003c-- 2b\n└── Provider definitions \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:35\n    ├── Claude: ~/.claude.json \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:36\n    ├── Codex: ~/.codex/config.toml \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:45\n    └── OpenCode: ~/.config/opencode/... \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:52",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nDevelopers using multiple AI coding assistants (Claude, Cursor, Factory, etc.) face a **fragmented configuration nightmare**. Each tool stores its MCP servers, skills, and settings in different formats and locations - JSON vs TOML, ~/.claude vs ~/.config, different key names for the same concept. This forces users to manually duplicate configurations and maintain consistency across tools, wasting time and causing errors.\n\n## Details\n\nThe provider config system solves this with a **unified abstraction layer**. It defines a registry of supported providers [2b], each with their specific config paths and formats. When you want to add an MCP server, you call `add-mcp` [2c] once, and the system automatically translates and writes the configuration to all target providers in their native formats.\n\nThe core flow works like this: the main run function [2a] receives your operation, routes it to the appropriate handler, which then loads the target provider's config file, manipulates the data structure (like adding to the mcpServers map [2d]), and persists it back using the correct format [2e]. The system handles all the ugly details - JSON vs TOML serialization, different file locations, varying key names - so you don't have to.\n\nThis enables powerful workflows like syncing MCP servers from Claude to all other tools with a single command, or exporting/importing entire configurations as backups."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "title": "MCP Skill Installation and Generation",
      "description": "System that introspects MCP servers and automatically generates foxctl skills for all available tools",
      "locations": [
