        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Skill Execution Framework\n├── CLI Command Entry \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skills_run.go:21\n│   └── newSkillsRunCommand() \u003c-- 4a\n│       └── findSkill() discovery \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skills_run.go:54\n│           └── executeSkill() \u003c-- 4c\n├── Skill Runtime Framework \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:81\n│   └── skillmain.Main() generic entry \u003c-- 4e\n│       ├── Bootstrap context \u0026 config \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:87\n│       ├── Parse \u0026 validate JSON input \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:140\n│       └── Call skill.run() \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:167\n└── Individual Skill Implementation \u003c-- skills/mcp_bridge/main.go:48\n    └── mcp_bridge.run() or providers.run() \u003c-- skills/providers/main.go:150\n        └── Business logic execution \u003c-- 4d",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nThe foxctl system needed a **unified way to execute skills** while supporting multiple AI coding assistants (Claude, Codex, OpenCode, Factory, Gemini). Each skill is a standalone binary with its own manifest, but they all need consistent input parsing, validation, error handling, and execution context. Without this framework, every skill would need to reimplement boilerplate for JSON parsing, path validation, session management, and error formatting.\n\n# Details\n\nThe skill execution framework provides a **standardized runtime** that all skills inherit. When you run `foxctl skills run mcp/bridge`, the CLI command [4a] discovers the skill binary and manifest, then executes it with merged input parameters [4c]. The skill's main function uses the generic `skillmain.Main()` entry point [4e] which handles the complex bootstrap process: loading configuration, parsing JSON input, validating parameters, and setting up the execution context with workspace and session information.\n\nEach skill only needs to implement a simple `run()` function that receives the validated input and a rich `RunContext` object containing CAS storage, path validation, logging, and other services [4d]. This design lets skills focus on their **core business logic** while the framework handles cross-cutting concerns like security (path validation), observability (structured logging), and integration (session management).\n\nThe framework also supports **multiple execution modes** - ephemeral for fast execution, cached for repeated operations, and daemon-mode for persistent processes. This flexibility allows the same skill code to be used in different scenarios while maintaining consistent behavior and error handling across the entire foxctl ecosystem."
    }
  ],
  "description": "This codemap traces the MCP bridging and provider config management systems in foxctl, showing how the [1a] MCP bridge enables communication with any MCP server, how [2b] provider configurations are managed across multiple AI assistants, how [3b] MCP tools are discovered and converted to foxctl skills, and how [4c] these skills integrate into the broader execution framework.",
